ntion weakness (memory), and communication issues (nasty remarks). Those are serious.\n\nCandidate D has responsibility, problem solving, attention, stress resilience, but lacks reliability evidence, and has crew cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper). Also unknown adaptability.\n\nCandidate A has technical competence and decision anticipation, leadership, but unknown reliability and stress resilience, and negative traits (show-off, not open to new ideas). Lacks evidence for many criteria.\n\nThus, Candidate C seems strongest overall despite communication concerns. However, Agent 3 argued for B, citing that communication issues for B are more trainable than C's egocentric. We need to respond. We can either agree with Agent 3, or argue that C's reliability and decision quality outweigh B's communication issues. We need to provide evidence.\n\nWe have explicit facts: C 100% reliable, correct decisions quickly, positive crew atmosphere. B: very conscientious, handles stress well, but nasty remarks. Which is more critical? Reliability and decision quality are top. Communication is also critical. But we can argue that C's positive crew atmosphere suggests good cooperation despite communication difficulty. Also, egocentric may be mitigated.\n\nThus, we could maintain recommendation for C, or shift to B. Let's decide. I think C still best overall. Provide argument: reliability and decision quality are non-negotiable; communication issues are concerning but less than lack of reliability. Also, C's positive crew atmosphere indicates some cooperation. B's nasty remarks could be more severe.\n\nThus, we will vote for Candidate C, and argue that despite communication concerns, C's reliability, decision quality, stress resilience, and positive crew atmosphere make him the best fit. Also note that we still need more info on A and D, but currently C leads.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 1 recommended C, Agent 3 recommended B. We'll say we align with Agent 1,